Build the Gen3 driver (i915) without LLVM.

Mesa asks for LLVM whenever i915 is built: the GMA 900 to 3150 have no
vertex shader hardware, so vertices are shaded on the CPU by Mesa's draw
module, which is fastest with LLVM's compiler. LLVM is 100 MB of library,
on a netbook with 1 GB of memory whose whole system runs from memory, and
it compiles each shader when it is first used, which on an Atom is a
visible stall.

The draw module has an interpreter too (draw_vs_exec.c, which softpipe runs
its vertex shaders through in every pack), and takes the driver's shaders
through it when LLVM is absent. Nothing in the driver itself uses LLVM. A
window manager draws a few thousand vertices a frame, which the interpreter
does in a millisecond or two.

Only the requirement is removed. A pack built with LLVM enabled is as
upstream has it.

--- a/meson.build	2026-11-25 11:43:15.529287989 +0200
+++ b/meson.build	2026-11-25 11:43:15.531232689 +0200
@@ -226,7 +226,6 @@
 endforeach
 draw_with_llvm = with_llvm.allowed() and get_option('draw-use-llvm')
 with_llvm = with_llvm \
-  .enable_if(with_gallium_i915, error_message : 'i915 Gallium driver requires LLVM for vertex shaders') \
   .enable_if(with_gallium_llvmpipe, error_message : 'LLVMPipe Gallium driver requires LLVM') \
   .enable_if(with_gallium_r300 and draw_with_llvm, error_message : 'R300 Gallium driver requires LLVM for vertex shaders on IGP parts') \
   .enable_if(with_gallium_radeonsi and amd_with_llvm, error_message : 'RadeonSI Gallium driver configured to require LLVM')
